#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:0
msgid ""
"Schedule automatic snap refreshes, pause or resume updates, manage metered "
"connections, run manual updates, and revert snaps to earlier revisions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:8
msgid "Manage updates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:10
msgid ""
"Snaps update automatically, and by default, the *snapd* daemon checks for "
"updates 4 times a day. Each update check is called a **refresh**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:12
msgid ""
"Updates can be set to occur on Friday at midnight, or for specific days of "
"the month, such as only the third Monday, or even the last Friday of the "
"month, between 23:00 to 01:00 the next day. They can even be postponed "
"indefinitely, or for a set period of time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:14
msgid ""
"When, if, and how often, these updates occur can is the job of the *snap "
"refresh* command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:17
msgid ""
"Snaps running in {ref}`_devmode_ <explanation-security-snap-confinement>`, "
"or installed locally, are typically intended for testing and do not update "
"automatically until they've been published and downloaded from the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:20
msgid "Refresh update control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:22
msgid ""
"There are two general approaches to postponing or otherwise managing snap "
"updates, with  either the `snap refresh --hold` command, or with system "
"settings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:24
msgid "Pause or stop updates with refresh hold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:25
#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:105
msgid "Control updates with system options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:27
msgid "Pause or stop automatic updates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:29
msgid "**Requires: snapd 2.58+**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:31
msgid ""
"The `snap refresh --hold` command holds, or postpones, snap updates for "
"individual snaps, or for all snaps on the system, either indefinitely or for "
"a specified period of time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:33
msgid "snap refresh --hold=<duration> <snap1> <snap2>...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:37
msgid ""
"Time duration units can be seconds (s), minutes (m) or hours (h), or a "
"combination of these.  To postpone updates indefinitely, a value of "
"`forever` is also valid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:39
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh --hold=24h firefox\n"
"General refreshes of \"firefox\" held until 2022-10-26T14:10:53+01:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:44
msgid "If no duration is specified, the time duration defaults to `forever`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:47
msgid ""
"If no snaps are specified, the hold applies to all snaps installed on the "
"system:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:49
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh --hold=24h\n"
"Auto-refresh of all snaps held until 2022-10-26T14:25:58+01:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:54
msgid ""
"To see which snaps are being held, look for `held` in the _notes_ column "
"when running `snap list`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:56
msgid ""
"$ snap list\n"
"Name         Version  Rev   Tracking       Publisher          "
"Notes                         \n"
"alacritty    0.8.0    46    latest/stable  snapcrafters       classic   \n"
"vlc          3.0.18   3078  latest/stable  videolan✓          "
"-                              \n"
"yt-dlp       18       212   latest/edge    morrisong          held      \n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:64
msgid ""
"However, there are important differences in how a hold is applied, depending "
"on whether individual snaps are specified or not. These differences are "
"described below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:66
msgid "If snaps are specified"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:68
#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:80
msgid "The refresh hold is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:69
msgid "**Effective on auto-refreshes and general snap refresh requests**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:70
msgid "**Not effective on targeted snap refreshes**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:72
msgid ""
"When one or more snaps are specified, the hold is effective only on their "
"auto-refreshes and general refresh requests from `snap refresh`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:74
msgid ""
"However, a refresh that targets a held snap specifically will not be blocked "
"and will always be able to proceed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:76
msgid ""
"This can be useful if a snap upgrade is known to be problematic. That "
"specific snap can be held while the remainder of the system is safely "
"refreshed. After the snap upgrade problem has been solved, and a new "
"revision of the snap published, that snap can then be manually refreshed as "
"a specific target. If the upgrade works as expected, the hold can be safely "
"removed (see {ref}`Remove a hold <ref-manage-updates_remove-a-hold>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:78
msgid "If no snaps are specified"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:81
msgid "**Effective only on auto-refreshes**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:82
msgid ""
"**Not effective on general snap refresh requests and targeted snap "
"refreshes**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:84
msgid ""
"If no snaps are specified, a hold applies to all snaps installed on the "
"system, however the hold is only effective on auto-refreshes and will not "
"block either general refresh requests from 'snap refresh', or specific snap "
"requests from 'snap refresh target-snap'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:87
msgid "Remove a hold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:89
msgid ""
"The `snap refresh --unhold` command removes a refresh hold, either for the "
"specified snaps or for all snaps when no snaps are targeted specifically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:91
msgid "For a single snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:93
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh --unhold firefox\n"
"Removed general refresh hold of \"firefox\"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:98
msgid "For for all snaps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:100
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh --unhold\n"
"Removed auto-refresh hold on all snaps\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:107
msgid ""
"The refresh rate is freely configurable, and can be set to any duration "
"above a lower bound of once every 20 minutes. There are four {ref}`system-"
"wide options <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-set-system-options>` that manage "
"how updates are handled:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:109
msgid "**refresh.timer**: defines the refresh frequency and schedule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:110
msgid ""
"**refresh.hold**: delays the next refresh until the defined time and date"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:111
msgid ""
"**refresh.metered**: pauses refresh updates when network connection is "
"metered"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:112
msgid ""
"**refresh.retain**: sets how many revisions of a snap are stored on the "
"system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:114
msgid "refresh.timer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:116
msgid ""
"Use *refresh.timer* to modify when, and how frequently, your snaps are "
"refreshed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:118
msgid ""
"The following example asks the system to only refresh snaps between  4.00am "
"and 7.00am, and 7.00pm and 10:10pm:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:120
msgid "sudo snap set system refresh.timer=4:00-7:00,19:00-22:10 \n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:124
msgid "Other examples for the time and frequency option include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "Result"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "`mon,10:00,,fri,15:00`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "Mondays at 10:00, Fridays at 15:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "`mon,fri,10:00,15:00`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "Mondays at 10:00 and 15:00, Fridays at 10:00 and 15:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "`mon-wed,fri,9:00-11:00/2`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "Monday to Wednesday and on Friday, twice between 9:00 and 11:10"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "`mon,9:00~11:00,,wed,22:00~23:00`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid ""
"Mondays, some time between 9:00 and 11:00, and on Wednesdays, some time "
"between 22:00 and 23:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "`mon,wed`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "Monday and on Wednesday, at 0:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "`mon2-wed,23:00-24:00`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid ""
"2nd Monday of the month, through the following Wednesday, between 23:00 and "
"24:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "`fri5,23:00-01:00`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:16
msgid "Last Friday of the month, from 23:00 to 1:00 the next day"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:136
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Timer string format <interfaces-timer-string-format>` for a "
"comprehensive breakdown of the syntax used to define times and frequencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:138
msgid ""
"You can check the update frequency for your environment with the `refresh` "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:140
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh --time\n"
"timer: 00:00~24:00/4\n"
"last: today at 07:47 BST\n"
"next: today at 12:13 BST\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:147
msgid ""
"By default, the snap system is scheduled to refresh four times per day, as "
"shown in the above output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:149
msgid "refresh.hold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:151
msgid ""
"Use *refresh.hold* to delay snap refreshes until a defined time and date (up "
"to 90 days). The time and date format needs to conform to [RFC 3339](https://"
"tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3339)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:153
msgid ""
"For example, *5:22pm (BST), Tuesday 23rd April 2019*, would look like the "
"following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:155
msgid "2019-04-23T17:22:54+01:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:158
msgid "The correct format can be generated with the *date* command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:160
msgid ""
"$ date --date=\"BST 2023-09-23 17:22:54\" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%:z\n"
"2023-09-23T17:22:54+01:00\n"
"\n"
"$ sudo snap set system refresh.hold=\"$(date --date=tomorrow +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:"
"%S%:z)\"\n"
"$ sudo snap get system refresh.hold\n"
"2019-04-24T17:22:54+01:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:169
msgid ""
"After a refresh, the next refresh can be delayed by up to 90 days, after "
"which a refresh will be performed regardless of the *refresh.hold* value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:171
msgid "refresh.metered"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:173
msgid ""
"Use *refresh.metered* to pause and re-enable the refresh process when  "
"*NetworkManager* detects a metered connection, such as an LTE link with a "
"limited data plan."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:175
msgid "To hold refreshing snaps when on a metered connection:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:177
msgid "sudo snap set system refresh.metered=hold\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:181
msgid "To allow refreshing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:183
msgid "sudo snap set system refresh.metered=null\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:187
msgid ""
"By default, *refresh.metered* is enabled when a metered connection is "
"detected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:189
msgid ""
"[quote] ⓘ  refresh.metered is available in snap 2.33 and later. [/quote]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:193
msgid "<a name=\"retain\"></a>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:193
msgid "<a name=\"retain\">"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:193
msgid "</a>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:195
msgid "refresh.retain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:197
msgid ""
"Use *refresh.retain* to set the maximum number of a snap's revisions stored "
"by the system *after* the next refresh:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:199
msgid "sudo snap set system refresh.retain=3\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:202
msgid ""
"The *refresh.retain* value can be a number between 2 and 20. The default is "
"`refresh.retain=3` on Ubuntu Core systems and `refresh.retain=2` on "
"*classic* Ubuntu systems, such as those running an Ubuntu LTS release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:204
msgid "[quote] ⓘ  refresh.retain is available in snap 2.34 and later. [/quote]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:208
msgid "Manual updates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:210
msgid ""
"Regardless of when a refresh is scheduled, a refresh can be initiated with "
"the `snap refresh` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:212
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh\n"
"gnome-system-monitor 3.28.2 from 'canonical' refreshed\n"
"gnome-calculator 3.28.2 from 'canonical' refreshed\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:217
msgid ""
"The *refresh* command can also be used to see when the last refresh occurred "
"and when the next is scheduled:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:219
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh --time\n"
"timer: 00:00~24:00/4\n"
"last: today at 09:16 GMT\n"
"next: today at 17:39 GMT\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:226
msgid ""
"The first line in the above output shows the value of the *timer* system "
"option. This defines how and when a refresh should be scheduled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:228
msgid ""
"To see which snaps are going to be updated with the next *refresh*, use the "
"additional `--list` argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:230
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh --list\n"
"Name           Version                    Rev   Publisher     Notes\n"
"core           16-2.45.1+git2022.b6b3c25  9584  canonical✓    core\n"
"get-iplayer    3.26                       250   snapcrafters  -\n"
"qt551          5.5                        30    keshavnrj     -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:238
msgid "Locally installed snaps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:240
msgid ""
"The `snap refresh --amend` command can be used to replace a locally "
"installed snap with an identically named snap on the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:242
msgid "Monitor changes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:244
msgid ""
"Use the `snap changes` and `snap tasks <change-id>` commands to see details "
"about what changed during the last refresh:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:246
msgid ""
"$ snap changes\n"
"ID    Status  Spawn                   Ready                   Summary\n"
"2052  Done    today at 09:34 BST      today at 09:35 BST      Auto-refresh 7 "
"snaps\n"
"2053  Done    today at 15:16 BST      today at 15:17 BST      Refresh snaps "
"\"gnome-calculator\", \"flock-chat\", \"gnome-characters\", \"gnome-system-"
"monitor\"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:253
msgid "Add the _change-id_ to see what actions a specific change performed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:255
msgid ""
"$ snap tasks 2053\n"
"Status  Spawn               Ready               Summary\n"
"Done    today at 15:16 BST  today at 15:16 BST  Ensure prerequisites for "
"\"gnome-calculator\" are available\n"
"Done    today at 15:16 BST  today at 15:16 BST  Download snap \"gnome-"
"calculator\" (199) from channel \"stable\"\n"
"Done    today at 15:16 BST  today at 15:16 BST  Fetch and check assertions "
"for snap \"gnome-calculator\" (199)\n"
"⋮\n"
"Done    today at 15:16 BST  today at 15:16 BST  Start snap \"gnome-system-"
"monitor\" (54) services\n"
"Done    today at 15:16 BST  today at 15:16 BST  Clean up \"gnome-system-"
"monitor\" (54) install\n"
"Done    today at 15:16 BST  today at 15:16 BST  Run configure hook of "
"\"gnome-system-monitor\" snap if present\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:267
msgid ""
"Optionally, the `--format` flag may be passed with the `json` argument to "
"return the data as JSON rather than a tabular representation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:269
msgid "Revert to an earlier revision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:271
msgid ""
"A snap may be reverted to an earlier revision with the `snap revert` "
"command. By default, it will attempt to revert to the previous revision:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:273
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap revert vlc\n"
"vlc reverted to 3.0.5-1\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:278
msgid ""
"The optional `--revision` argument can be specified to request a specific "
"revision:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:280
msgid "snap revert vlc --revision 500\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:284
msgid ""
"This operation will revert both the snap revision and the configuration data "
"associated with the software. If the previously used revision of the snap is "
"from a different channel, that snap will be installed but the channel being "
"tracked won’t change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:286
msgid ""
"User data, such as data generated by the snap and stored in a database, is "
"often stored in a *common* directory and will not be reverted. See {ref}"
"`Data locations <interfaces-data-locations>` for more details on what "
"information is stored and where."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:288
msgid ""
"A snap won’t automatically update to a version previously reverted from, and "
"the output from `snap refresh` will continue to state *All snaps up to "
"date*. A reverted snap will be automatically updated when a new and "
"different revision is made available by the publisher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:290
msgid ""
"However, explicitly adding the snap name to `snap refresh` *will* update the "
"snap, regardless of whether the latest revision was previously reverted from "
"or not:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:292
msgid ""
"$ snap list --all vlc\n"
"Name  Version  Rev  Tracking  Publisher  Notes\n"
"vlc   3.0.5-1  768  stable    videolan✓  -\n"
"vlc   3.0.6    770  stable    videolan✓  disabled\n"
"$ sudo snap refresh\n"
"All snaps up to date.\n"
"$ sudo snap refresh vlc\n"
"vlc 3.0.6 from VideoLAN✓ refreshed\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:303
msgid ""
"A previously used snap that was reverted from will display *disabled* in the "
"Notes column of the output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/manage-updates.md:305
msgid ""
"**Reverting to a previous version of *snapd*** </br> The snapd snap manages "
"the snap packaging system, and is therefore a special case if you wish to "
"revert it, or downgrade the currently running version. It must be reverted "
"on its own, and there cannot be any pending changes for other snaps in the "
"pipeline."
